1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,140
Alex wants to watch one documentary.

2
00:00:02,980 --> 00:00:04,780
Seven days free? Perfect.

3
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:07,760
A few taps, a payment card, and he's in.

4
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:10,980
He tells himself he'll cancel before the
trial ends.

5
00:00:11,460 --> 00:00:13,460
Future Alex has this under control.

6
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:16,160
Cut to breakfast, a month later.

7
00:00:16,740 --> 00:00:17,680
His phone lights up.

8
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:20,780
Nineteen dollars and ninety-nine cents.

9
00:00:20,780 --> 00:00:23,280
Alex stares at the charge, then at his
coffee.

10
00:00:24,140 --> 00:00:26,180
Apparently, future Alex was busy.

11
00:00:26,940 --> 00:00:29,140
No problem. Open the app.

12
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:30,260
Find settings.

13
00:00:31,020 --> 00:00:34,180
Find the account. Find something called
membership benefits.

14
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:36,740
He came here to cancel a subscription.

15
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:40,360
Somehow, he's now reading about exclusive
features.

16
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:44,140
Joining felt like walking through an open
door.

17
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:49,120
Leaving feels like navigating a building
where someone keeps moving the exit

18
00:00:49,120 --> 00:00:49,540
signs.

19
00:00:50,420 --> 00:00:54,040
Why can a free trial be so much easier to
start than to stop?

20
00:00:54,950 --> 00:00:59,120
A trial and a subscription can be the same
arrangement at different prices.

21
00:00:59,940 --> 00:01:01,640
The initial period costs nothing.

22
00:01:02,540 --> 00:01:06,380
Then recurring billing starts under the
terms you accepted, unless you

23
00:01:06,380 --> 00:01:07,280
cancel in time.

24
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:10,860
The important decision comes after the
exciting one.

25
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:14,020
On signup day, the benefit is immediate.

26
00:01:15,020 --> 00:01:17,400
The cancellation task belongs to next
week.

27
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:22,280
Then next week arrives with work,
groceries, and a password you can't

28
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:25,520
remember. Forgetting isn't proof you
wanted another month.

29
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:28,520
But the billing system may keep running
anyway.

30
00:01:29,540 --> 00:01:31,360
Automatic renewal can be useful.

31
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,540
You probably don't want your music to stop
halfway through a road trip

32
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:37,440
because you forgot to renew it.

33
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,860
The problem is when continuing becomes
effortless while stopping becomes

34
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:45,660
confusing, or when important terms are
hard to notice.

35
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:48,960
A password check can protect your account.

36
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:51,940
A clear confirmation can prevent mistakes.

37
00:01:52,760 --> 00:01:54,600
Not every extra step is deceptive.

38
00:01:55,460 --> 00:01:59,580
What matters is whether the process helps
you carry out your decision, or

39
00:01:59,580 --> 00:02:01,840
repeatedly steers you away from it.

40
00:02:02,660 --> 00:02:03,360
Back to Alex.

41
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:05,080
First comes the search.

42
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:10,220
A cancellation option can be buried
beneath menus, labels, and help pages.

43
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:12,100
Each screen is small.

44
00:02:12,980 --> 00:02:17,180
Together, they turn a simple intention
into a task that needs time and

45
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:17,460
attention.

46
00:02:18,380 --> 00:02:19,680
Then comes the offer.

47
00:02:20,420 --> 00:02:21,440
Half off your next month.

48
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,640
That might be a good deal if you actually
want the service.

49
00:02:25,420 --> 00:02:26,680
But Alex wanted to leave.

50
00:02:27,380 --> 00:02:30,880
Now he has another decision to make before
he can finish the first one.

51
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:32,940
Next, two buttons.

52
00:02:33,740 --> 00:02:34,800
One is big and bright.

53
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:36,880
The other is quiet and gray.

54
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:41,480
Continue might mean continue canceling, or
continue your membership.

55
00:02:42,220 --> 00:02:46,020
Alex has to read the action, instead of
trusting whichever button looks

56
00:02:46,020 --> 00:02:47,080
easiest to press.

57
00:02:47,840 --> 00:02:49,800
Then the benefits parade begins.

58
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:52,000
You'll lose access to this.

59
00:02:52,540 --> 00:02:53,460
You'll miss out on that.

60
00:02:53,980 --> 00:02:55,580
Those details can be useful.

61
00:02:56,440 --> 00:03:00,460
But a long parade can pull the
conversation away from one basic question:

62
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:03,180
do you want to pay for another billing
period?

63
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:05,800
Alex gets a work message.

64
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:09,200
He closes the cancellation screen halfway
through.

65
00:03:09,860 --> 00:03:11,160
He hasn't decided to stay.

66
00:03:11,820 --> 00:03:13,120
He also hasn't finished leaving.

67
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:17,900
If renewal is the default, an unfinished
cancellation can leave

68
00:03:17,900 --> 00:03:19,060
the subscription active.

69
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:24,520
The Federal Trade Commission has
documented cancellation obstacles and

70
00:03:24,520 --> 00:03:27,620
confusing interface choices in its work on
dark patterns.

71
00:03:28,580 --> 00:03:32,280
These are designs that can undermine a
person's intended choice.

72
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:34,100
Our maze is fictional;

73
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:39,000
it illustrates the pattern, not a claim
about every subscription company.

74
00:03:39,780 --> 00:03:42,380
Sometimes the problem is the wrong door.

75
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:45,860
The app you use and the company billing
you may be different.

76
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:49,940
A subscription purchased through an app
store can have its cancellation

77
00:03:49,940 --> 00:03:52,560
controls in that store's subscription
settings.

78
00:03:53,260 --> 00:03:55,380
Check the receipt before chasing buttons.

79
00:03:56,620 --> 00:03:59,060
Dragging an app into the trash can feel
satisfying.

80
00:04:00,220 --> 00:04:02,220
It also may leave the subscription
running.

81
00:04:02,920 --> 00:04:07,360
For subscriptions billed through Google
Play, uninstalling the app does not

82
00:04:07,360 --> 00:04:09,800
cancel them. The icon is gone.

83
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:12,560
The payment arrangement can still be
there.

84
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:15,260
So Alex changes his approach.

85
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:20,320
He opens the receipt, checks who is
billing him, and follows that provider's

86
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:21,880
official cancellation instructions.

87
00:04:22,780 --> 00:04:23,400
Less wandering.

88
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:25,040
A clearer destination.

89
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,840
If the subscription isn't listed, he
checks whether he used another account.

90
00:04:31,020 --> 00:04:35,140
Before his next trial, he checks the
renewal price, billing frequency,

91
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:37,560
deadline, and cancellation method.

92
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:42,020
He puts a reminder ahead of the deadline,
with enough room to handle a

93
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:42,400
problem.

94
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:46,020
The reminder includes a link and the
account he used.

95
00:04:46,020 --> 00:04:49,000
Canceling early might be an option.

96
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:50,960
But check the terms first.

97
00:04:51,780 --> 00:04:53,800
Some trials end access when you cancel.

98
00:04:54,880 --> 00:04:57,300
Others keep access until an expiration
date.

99
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,200
Don't assume that canceling today always
means keeping every remaining day

100
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:02,820
for free.

101
00:05:03,660 --> 00:05:07,120
This time, Alex follows the process all
the way through.

102
00:05:07,780 --> 00:05:09,960
He looks for the actual cancellation
confirmation,

103
00:05:10,780 --> 00:05:14,440
checks the effective date, and saves the
email or screenshot.

104
00:05:15,540 --> 00:05:19,420
Reaching a page about cancellation isn't
the same as completing it.

105
00:05:20,360 --> 00:05:25,020
He also separates two questions: have
future renewals stopped, and can the

106
00:05:25,020 --> 00:05:26,920
charge already made be refunded?

107
00:05:27,420 --> 00:05:28,540
Those are different requests.

108
00:05:28,540 --> 00:05:33,080
Refund eligibility depends on the service,
the circumstances,

109
00:05:33,080 --> 00:05:37,800
and applicable rules. Canceling doesn't
automatically settle both.

110
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,920
If the provider keeps charging after a
completed cancellation,

111
00:05:42,920 --> 00:05:45,260
Alex keeps his records and contacts
support.

112
00:05:46,120 --> 00:05:49,320
For charges he believes are unauthorized
or incorrect,

113
00:05:50,160 --> 00:05:53,120
he contacts his card issuer promptly about
options.

114
00:05:53,880 --> 00:05:56,720
He can also report deceptive practices to
the FTC.

115
00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:01,480
A useful subscription can absolutely earn
its place.

116
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:04,940
Alex keeps his music service because he
uses it.

117
00:06:05,400 --> 00:06:08,760
He cancels the documentary app because the
movie is over.

118
00:06:09,420 --> 00:06:11,080
The goal isn't to cancel everything.

119
00:06:11,940 --> 00:06:14,800
It's to make the bill reflect a decision
he actually made.

120
00:06:15,920 --> 00:06:18,760
The best time to find the exit is before
you walk in.

121
00:06:19,460 --> 00:06:22,340
A free trial isn't only a chance to test
the product.

122
00:06:22,340 --> 00:06:27,120
It's also a chance to ask: what happens
next, and how do I leave?

123
00:06:27,740 --> 00:06:29,400
Future you deserves an answer.
